#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425932} :activity/id #uuid "c1b2bf8a-6e24-5f4d-b8cf-9b6296b332cb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dc624a-6195-52a9-9917-f3684e4a574f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425913} {:answer/id #uuid "4a78a72b-b4d7-5293-8aba-7c35212132ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425914} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3e8aa2-20ed-5607-9c37-0e3dceaf9028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425915} {:answer/id #uuid "9f70c45e-aafb-568b-ad0e-18cd0c73be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425919} {:answer/id #uuid "f078a363-807c-53b3-b111-d4c6e7e91d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425915} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a9dd81-ee99-5b34-b7e2-398216f26e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19ad1761-3136-5be8-b038-5aa62265f48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80526" :question/problem "![](images/88/8866056ce5c7c80ed8452246cc3a40074b6841615ed6ef472e7a1612709e51fa.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,1]\\times [-3,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4e98cf8b-1d7c-55a2-90e4-587c132c726b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16080a41-2e2f-51fa-a9c4-12eb38603975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351762} {:answer/id #uuid "74bad7f9-9b45-59d6-9532-f075ca6e95ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352591} {:answer/id #uuid "8a40d22c-1bca-53d5-b4c4-4e579271dd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352772} {:answer/id #uuid "d925b33c-0df5-5c5b-9a60-d057d993edef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353455} {:answer/id #uuid "e226575c-1f65-5f96-b71a-27515a394a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351762} :answer-field/id #uuid "13869e20-bad1-5d54-bcbb-82ddee104a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa4fe275-03b1-57bc-8327-4434c307d887" :question/math-academy-id "q-86899" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-|x|+2$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/f3/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "65ccb889-1cb9-5b78-a946-1218d327ccf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e995b4f-80df-5a44-ac52-ef3e8528fb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425916} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea449c7-e858-51b4-bdf2-6beadec281c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425918} {:answer/id #uuid "c738fcf4-81ab-523b-a4a3-3c52b0e51091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425920} {:answer/id #uuid "e52b4a76-97bf-58a6-8111-6703e511f2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425922} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb50184-5f7d-5a30-8c1d-9227e10a07fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425916} :answer-field/id #uuid "30306495-7925-535a-81a1-49a15747abcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1654ded7-c0bc-579c-a9e9-6daa6a1243e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86894" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d8f57afa16e99c13e6e36c5544a7c7594da13321bd3d01692a5a296a20953d7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/ce/ce9b9f3435a94b3f9f87b195e5b1591c80281b23cfb76ceb8e79cdeec3ae66b0.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "669eeb99-fe93-5bc0-a8ac-52fd38d35e53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b1980b-e87f-5251-b68d-f7b2a5280848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352187} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd2e348-e399-5345-96ea-c3ac2b25e252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352672} {:answer/id #uuid "a578f3f2-5df9-51d4-b3ac-e913a144510c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352977} {:answer/id #uuid "abf9fc1e-1781-5aab-b380-3abe698af5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353026} {:answer/id #uuid "bd863838-ce95-5779-b5d7-f63609482fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352672} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe6f8d1-0f2d-5248-98d5-e4c5850a61a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b045a52-4215-5bdb-9dcf-5641984e3591" :question/math-academy-id "q-86898" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eedab041-fe80-5aa7-8ce9-3144bd5d6aa6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034daae9-6479-5df1-9b65-7e6139e7a49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425911} {:answer/id #uuid "204a2417-b1ee-5a94-9387-92210e84c9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425912} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd19b67-41ce-5d20-9075-74172011db9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425917} {:answer/id #uuid "daf363de-8d31-5c81-8ebc-62bc6b37b91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425921} {:answer/id #uuid "fa68e695-7f74-50ec-acaf-b9b8df374c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425921} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b56bb6c-74dd-5bd4-a9cd-cbed30098ff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c04d4c5-9913-59ea-a83f-46d8bb7c02cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129893" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfc135232008824ae0522bcc8454c5f97c2dfd13fb6e333e146a040ccbc1f22.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statements II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f7de3cb3-0140-51ce-a3e4-58a349b9b853"}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425910}]}
